{
  "requirement": "The book preprocessor MUST escape requirement names and link targets when inserting badge HTML. It MUST encode badge label and message text in Shields.io URL path components so punctuation cannot change the badge URL structure.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The reviewed tests adequately check `requirements.references.safe_badges`.\n\n**Obligations**\n1. The preprocessor MUST escape requirement names and link targets when it inserts badge HTML.\n2. It MUST encode badge label and message text in Shields.io URL path components, so punctuation cannot change the URL structure.\n\n**Obligation 2: Shields.io URL encoding**\n- `badge_urls_percent_encode_punctuation_and_html_is_escaped` calls `shield_badge` with punctuation in both label and message. The label is `a/b?c`. The message contains `#`, `%`, `&`, `<`, `>`, `\"`, `'`, a space and a non-ASCII character.\n- It asserts the exact URL: `a%2Fb%3Fc-x%23y%25z%26w%3Cv%3E%22u%27_caf%C3%A9-006ba8?style=...&amp;labelColor=555`. Those assertions cover the structural characters `/`, `?`, `#`, `%` and `&`, plus percent-encoding of non-ASCII text.\n- The same test asserts that the `alt` text is HTML-escaped.\n- `shields_badge_urls_escape_requirement_names` covers the Shields-specific escaping of `_` to `__`, `-` to `--` and space to `_`, in both a requirement-name message and a status-style message.\n- `replace_query_directives` and `reference_badge` both go through `shield_badge`, so these unit tests cover every badge URL built there.\n\n**Obligation 1: escaping names and link targets**\n- `reference_link_targets_are_escaped` covers `reference_badge`, which handles `{cite}` in prose. The target chapter's rendered path contains `&`, `\"`, `<` and `>`. The test asserts the escaped `href` and that no raw `\"q\"` or `<x>` appears in the guide.\n- `definition_evidence_link_targets_are_escaped` covers `replace_definitions`. It asserts the escaped detail-page `href` on the name badge for a chapter path containing special characters. It also asserts the title text and that the raw characters are absent.\n- Inside `replace_definitions`, `escaped_name`, the anchor and the permalink are built from names that `valid_name` has already admitted. A comment in the code says valid names contain no HTML-special characters, so no test input can reach that escaping. Invalid names remain literal, so this path has no distinguishable behaviour to test.\n\nNo gap found, and there are no acknowledgments.",
  "references": "f360114568ba3231c5da9e9b2c91d05137e8af13c41c17d23dea91167c755e21",
